  "references": [
    {
      "path": "assets-source/refs/road_tunnel_portal/ref-1.jpg",
      "sha256": "dca38a6a7c5988c54b23a4085ffdd6364602360868ccd9f248e14a9a384b6b58",
      "sourceSha256": "08cf40a190ba894a513d776421464fdf0c245a38153b1222a6f19b5fdd9d16ca",
      "provider": "higgsfield",
      "model": "nano_banana_pro",
      "jobId": "1eba1a24-56c3-46c3-95b5-351a5654c02d",
      "prompt": "Tunnel portal tile: a single regular hexagonal diorama tile with exactly six straight flat edges, centred in frame, 32 m across, lush meadow grass cap and the hexagon side walls show a thin grass lip over dark root-filled topsoil that darkens softly downward into a near-black foot, one continuous dark earth, flat-topped so one flat side faces the camera along the bottom of the frame. The whole grass cap slopes up as one broad smooth grassy hillside from the bottom flat side, level with the road, to the top flat side about 4 m higher, like a tilted board. A 6 m wide two-lane asphalt country road, faded dashed white centre line, low concrete kerbs, grass verges with tufts and small bushes runs straight from the middle of the bottom flat side to the middle of the top flat side and stays level while the ground rises around it, so it runs through a grassy cutting whose steep banks grow taller toward the back. Along the top flat side, where the hillside is highest, the road enters an arched stone tunnel portal set into the raised face of the hill: a head wall of rough-faced blocks of weathered pale grey granite speckled with darker grey flecks, with a few warm buff fieldstones among them, laid in neat level courses in pale lime mortar, every block a little irregular with softly worn rounded edges and its own slight tone, patches of pale green-grey and mustard-yellow lichen, dark green moss in the joints and thickest along the foot of the wall, and faint darker rain streaks running down from under the coping, the stone reading as a matte surface, an elliptical arch over the road ringed by pale dressed granite voussoirs with a keystone, a pale dressed coping along the top of the wall level with the hilltop, the meadow turf of the hill runs over the top of the wall and its long grass and a few small ferns tumble over the front edge of the coping, and the steep banks of the road cutting are lush and uneven, with grass tufts, a few wildflowers, small ferns and dock leaves growing up against the foot of the wall, the opening a shadowed recess whose stone soffit fades to near-black a short way inside, with no view through, and a pair of small black iron wall lanterns either side of the arch. The rest of the tile is open meadow grass with a few wildflowers, stylized-real diorama miniature, real PBR materials, slightly exaggerated proportions, soft noon sun, neutral grey studio background, high camera looking down at about 65 degrees elevation so all six hexagon edges read clearly, the dark earth side walls visible along the front"
    },
